## Question 13

A 25-year-old male presents to the emergency department after being struck under the chin during a basketball game. He reports severe pain in front of his ears and has jaw locked in closed position. On physical examination, his jaw appears to be displaced posteriorly. Which ligament is most likely compromised due to this injury?

- A. Stylomandibular ligament
- B. Sphenomandibular ligament
- C. Lateral pterygoid muscle
- **D. Temporomandibular ligament** (Correct Answer)
- E. Sternoclavicular ligament

**Correct Answer:** D

**Explanation:**

The Temporomandibular joint (TMJ):

The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is a hinge type synovial joint that connects the mandible to the temporal bone of the skull. It is responsible for the movement of the jaw, allowing functions such as chewing, speaking, and yawning.

Movements of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) Hinge movement: Opening and closing the mouth. Gliding movement: Forward (protrusion) and backward (retrusion) movements of the jaw. Lateral movements: Side-to-side jaw motion. Muscles Involved:

Temporalis: Elevates and retracts the jaw.

Masseter: Elevates the jaw.

Medial and Lateral Pterygoid: Assist in side-to-side movements and protrusion.

Ligaments of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ):

Lateral capsular (Temporomandibular) ligament: Prevents posterior and inferior dislocation of the TMJ. It is the strongest ligament in the joint and reinforces the lateral side of the joint capsule. Sphenomandibular ligament originates from the sphenoid spine, and attaches to the mandible. Acts as a passive support to the mandible, especially when the mouth is wide open.

Stylomandibular ligament: Provides stability and limits excessive jaw movements. Overstretching can result in hypermobility of the jaw, increasing the risk of dislocation.

Innervation:The TMJ is primarily innervated by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (V3).

Clinical pearls:

Temporomandibular joint (TMJ) Dislocation:

Anterior dislocation:The most common form, where the condyle slips forward over the articular eminence, locking the mouth open. Trauma or excessive opening of the mouth (e.g., yawning, dental procedures) can stretch or tear ligaments, resulting in dislocation.

Posterior dislocation:Posterior temporomandibular joint (TMJ) dislocation is a rare condition compared to anterior dislocation. In this type of dislocation, the mandibular condyle is displaced backward, towards the external auditory canal and the base of the skull. Jaw locked in a closed position, In contrast to anterior dislocation, Patients are unable to close their mouths.

