## Question 8

A 52-year-old male presents to ED complaining of dizziness and discomfort. After initial assessment at triage his heart rate is found to be 40-60 bpm regular rhythm. ECG demonstrates a normal QRS complex with absent P waves. Which part of the conducting system is most likely acting as pacemaker in this patient?

- **A. Atrioventricular node** (Correct Answer)
- B. Purkinje fibers
- C. Bundle of His
- D. Bundle branches
- E. Sinoatrial node

**Correct Answer:** A

**Explanation:**

Conductive Pathway of Heart:

SA node initiates the cardiac muscle (pacemaker) -> Internodal fibers and AV node (delay impulse from the atria to ventricle) -> AV bundle branches -> Purkinje fibers (from apex to ventricles).

The sinoatrial node (SAN) is the primary pacemaker located in Right atrium. The SAN will normally discharge at a rate of 60-100 bpm. If the impulse generated in the Sa node is blocked, a group of cells further down the heart will become its pacemaker. The atrioventricular node (AVN) is the secondary pacemaker located between right atrium and ventricle near the atrial septum, and will normally discharge at about 40-60 beats per minute Purkinje fibers produce spontaneous action potential at rate of 20-40 bpm.

