## Question 3

A 20-week pregnant lady presents to the ED with exposure to chicken pox. What is your next step in management?

- A. Immediate reinforcing of vaccine
- B. No action required
- C. Immediate reinforcing of immunoglobulin
- D. Attend to antenatal clinic
- **E. Check her antibody status** (Correct Answer)

**Correct Answer:** E

**Explanation:**

Key Points:

If you have already had chickenpox, you have nothing to worry about.

Once you have had chickenpox you cannot catch it a second time, this is known as being immune. Nine out of ten UK adults are immune to chickenpox.

Chickenpox is very rare in pregnant women in the UK.

It affects very few babies in the womb and although it can be very serious in pregnancy st especially in 1 trimester in first 12 weeks (Recall) If you have been in contact with chickenpox and are not sure if you are immune, contact your GP or midwife and not to attend the antenatal clinic. If you have chickenpox, avoid contact with other pregnant women and new babies until at least five days after the rash appears, or until all the blisters have crusted over.

---

## Question 4

You are reviewing a patient with infected arteriovenous fistula and he will undergo hemofiltration tomorrow. Which of the following best describes the Transport of molecules across the membrane that occurs in haemofiltration?

- A. Osmosis
- B. Simple diffusion
- C. Active transport
- D. Facilitated diffusion
- **E. Ultrafiltration** (Correct Answer)

**Correct Answer:** E

**Explanation:**

Ultrafiltration refers to the process of movement of fluids through a membrane caused by a pressure gradient In dialysis, fluid is removed by ultrafiltration using the dialysis membrane. The pressure on the dialysate side is lower so water moves from the blood (place of higher pressure) to the dialysate (place of lower pressure). This is how the hemodialysis treatment removes fluid.

---

## Question 5

You are going to perform full cardiovascular examination in

a patient with known cardiac disease. Your consultant asks th how does the 4 heart is produced?

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> | 3' heart sound | Also Known as |
> |  | ventricular gallop |
> |  | caused by rapid flow |
> |  | of blood from the |
> |  | atria into the |
> |  | ventricles during the |
> |  | ventricular filling |
> | 4th heart sound | The fourth heart |
> |  | sound, S4, also |
> |  | known as 'atrial |
> |  | gallop' results from |
> |  | the contraction of the |
> |  | atria pushing blood |
> |  | into a stiff or |
> |  | hypertrophic |
> |  | ventricle, indicating |
> |  | failure of the left |
> |  | ventricle |

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> |  | contraction |
> | c wave | C: Contraction: |
> |  | lsovolumic |
> |  | contraction |
> |  | ventricular ejection |
> | Vv wave | V: Ventricular |
> |  | ejection followed by |
> |  | isovolumetric |
> |  | relaxation |

- A. Caused by closure of the semilunar (aortic and pulmonary) valves
- B. Caused by closure of the atrioventricular (mitral and tricuspid) valves
- C. Caused by rapid flow of blood from the atria into the ventricles during the ventricular filling
- D. Caused by filling of an abnormally stiff ventricle in atrial diastole
- **E. Caused by filling of an abnormally stiff ventricle in atrial systole** (Correct Answer)

**Correct Answer:** E

**Explanation:**

Note: Dicrotic notch on the arterial waveform represent closure of aortic valve (Recall) used to demarcate the end of systole and the beginning of diastole.

High Yield

The isovolumic relaxation phase starts when the aortic valve closes (Recall) and ends when the mitral valve opens. During this phase, the left ventricular pressure drops until it becomes lower than that of the left atrium. This allows opening of the atrioventricular valve and the filling of the ventricle.

