## Question 1

A 20-year-old woman underwent arthroscopy on her right ankle because of chronic ankle pain after a sprain. After arthroscopy, the patient complained of pain on the dorsum of her right foot and felt a radiating pain from the anterolateral portal to the dorsomedial aspect of her foot, this was most probably due to injury to which of the following nerve?

- A. Tibial nerve
- B. Deep fibular nerve
- C. Saphenous nerve
- **D. Superficial fibular nerve** (Correct Answer)
- E. Sural nerve

**Correct Answer:** D

**Explanation:**

Motor innervation of superficial fibular nerve

The superficial fibular nerve supplies the fibularis longus and the fibularis brevis. These muscles form the lateral compartment of the lower leg. They act to evert the foot (Recall) and thus act at the subtalar joint (eversion is the action of turningthe foot outwards).

Sensory Functions

Superficial peroneal nerve carries sensations from the anterolateral aspect of the leg and the greater part of the dorsum of the foot (except for the firstweb space).

