## Question 1

A 10 years old child admitted in hospital and being treated for DKA. In DKA treating complication cerebral edema, hypertonic saline is used. How hypertonic saline will decrease cerebral edema?

- A. K move out of the cell
- **B. Water moves out of the cells** (Correct Answer)
- C. Na and k move out of cells
- D. There is no net movement of water
- E. Water moves into the cell

**Correct Answer:** B

**Explanation:**

when hypertonic saline is administrated ECF becomes more concentrated compared with ICF. Osmotic potential draw movement of water molecules from inside to out of the cells.

Note: After heavy exercise when person drink water it increases intracellular fluid ICF volume (Recall) because water is hypotonic to body fluids dilute extracellular fluid relative to intracellular fluid, osmotic potential draws water into cells.

