## Question 6

A 30-year-old male unable to laterally rotate his arm following fracture of surgical neck of humerus, which of the following muscle most likely to be involved?

- A. Latissimus dorsi
- **B. Teres minor** (Correct Answer)
- C. Rhomboid major
- D. Supraspinatus
- E. Teres major

**Correct Answer:** B

**Explanation:**

The axillary nerve is most commonly damaged due to fracture of surgical neck of humerus, which supply the teres minor muscle which helps is lateral rotation of arm and also supply the deltoid muscle which cause abduction of arm...

Axillary nerve root value =C5 and C6 (RECALL).

---

## Question 7

A 20 years old male presents to the ED following stab wound to antecubital fossa with complaint of unable to flex his index and middle finger resulting in unopposed extension of index and middle finger. Which one of the following nerves is most likely injured?

- A. Radial nerve
- B. Ulnar nerve
- **C. Median nerve** (Correct Answer)
- D. Musculocutaneous nerve
- E. Axillary nerve

**Correct Answer:** C

**Explanation:**

common injuries affecting the median nerve include:

Supracondylar fracture of humerus Stab wound to antecubital fossa Compression of carpel tunnel syndrome (RECALL)

Median nerve innervation

Motor supply: all the anterior compartment of forearm muscles (Flexor compartment) (except flex or Carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus), the thenar muscles (flexor policies brevis, abductor pollicis brevis, opponent policies) and lateral two lumbricals are supplied by median nerve Sensory supply: lateral aspect of palm and palmar surface and fingertips of lateral three and half digits. (RECALL).

---

## Question 8

A 27-year-old man falls from a ladder. Neurological examination reveals an isolated defect of proprioception. Which tract is most likely injured?

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> | Dorsal column | Located: posteromedial aspect | Transmit ipsilateral proprioception, vibration |
> |  | of spinal cord | and light touch sensation |
> | Spinothalamic tract | Anterolateral aspect of cord | Transmit contralateral pain, crude touch |
> |  |  | and temperature sensation |
> | Lateral | Posterolateral aspect of cord | Control ipsilateral motor power |
> | corticospinal tract |  |  |

- A. Lateral spinothalamic
- B. Anterior spinothalamic
- **C. Dorsal column** (Correct Answer)
- D. Lateral corticospinal
- E. Posterior spinothalamic tract

**Correct Answer:** C


---

## Question 9

A 35 years old male presents to the ED with complaint of swelling in the right inguinal region. Regarding hernia which one of the following types protrudes through Hesselbach's triangle?

- A. Indirect inguinal hernia
- **B. Direct inguinal hernia** (Correct Answer)
- C. Paraumbilical hernia
- D. Spigelian hernia
- E. Femoral hernia

**Correct Answer:** B

**Explanation:**

Direct inguinal hernia lies medial to inferior epigastric vessels while indirect inguinal hernia lies lateral to inferior epigastric vessels. (RECALL)

Most common type of inguinal hernia=indirect inguinal hernia (Recall)

Most common side of inguinal hernia=Right side (RECALL)

The floor of the inguinal canal is the inguinal ligament, otherwise known as the Poupart ligament, which is formed by the external oblique aponeurosis (RECALL)

Type of hernia that enters the deep inguinal ring =indirect inguinal hernia (RECALL)

Type of hernia that protrudes through Hesselbach's triangle=Direct inguinal hernia (RECALL).

---

## Question 10

Regarding the blood supply of abdomen, superior mesenteric artery supply which one of the following structures?

