## Question 6

A 60-year-old male presents to the ED with complaint of fatigue. Lab value reveals anemia, Lymphocytosis, and thrombocytopenia. Which one of the following malignancies is most common in UK?

- A. CML
- B. Hodgkin lymphoma
- C. ALL
- D. AML
- **E. CLL** (Correct Answer)

**Correct Answer:** E

**Explanation:**

Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL) is the most common leukaemia in adults in the UK, particularly in the elderly (median age at diagnosis ~70 years). It accounts for ~40% of adult leukaemias. CLL is a B-cell malignancy presenting with lymphocytosis, lymphadenopathy, and hepatosplenomegaly.

---

## Question 7

A 52-year-old woman presents with a hoarse voice two weeks after neck surgery for a thyroid adenoma.

Which structure is most likely to have been injured during her surgery?

- A. Superior laryngeal nerve
- **B. Recurrent laryngeal nerve** (Correct Answer)
- C. Cricothyroid muscle
- D. Thyrohyoid muscle
- E. Vagus nerve

**Correct Answer:** B

**Explanation:**

The recurrent laryngeal nerve (RLN), a branch of the vagus nerve, is at risk during thyroidectomy. Unilateral injury causes hoarseness (ipsilateral vocal cord palsy). Bilateral injury causes stridor and airway obstruction. The RLN supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid (external branch of superior laryngeal nerve).

---

## Question 8

A 45-year-old man fell from a rooftop and presented with a head injury and hyperventilation. What could be the consequence of hyperventilation?

- **A. Decrease in cerebral blood flow** (Correct Answer)
- B. Increased in Pao2
- C. Increase in Pco2
- D. Decrease washout of Pco2
- E. Increase in cerebral blood flow

**Correct Answer:** A

**Explanation:**

Hyperventilation causes hypocapnia (low pCO2), which leads to cerebral vasoconstriction via reduced carbonic acid and alkalosis. This decreases cerebral blood flow. This is the physiological basis for using controlled hyperventilation as a temporising measure in raised intracranial pressure (ICP) -- but it can worsen cerebral ischaemia if prolonged.

---

## Question 9

A 15 years old male known case of sickle cell disease with a history of splenectomy 1 year back presented to the ED with complaint of bloody diarrhea. What is the most likely causative agent?

- **A. E-Coli** (Correct Answer)
- B. Hemophilus influenza
- C. Staph aureus
- D. Pseudomonas aeruginosa
- E. Neisseria meningitides

**Correct Answer:** A

**Explanation:**

E. coli (particularly EHEC O157:H7) is the leading cause of bloody diarrhoea (haemorrhagic colitis) and haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS). Post-splenectomy patients are particularly susceptible to encapsulated organisms, but E. coli causing bloody diarrhoea is a distinct epidemiological concern in this scenario.

---

## Question 10

Which one of the following stages of bone healing is followed by inflammation and proliferation?

- **A. Callus formation** (Correct Answer)
- B. Organization
- C. Consolidation
- D. Hematoma formation
- E. Remodeling

**Correct Answer:** A

**Explanation:**

Callus formation is the 3rd stage of fracture healing, following: 1) Haematoma formation (hours-days), 2) Inflammatory phase (days), 3) Soft callus/proliferative phase (weeks -- periosteal cells and fibroblasts proliferate), 4) Hard callus (woven bone formation), 5) Remodelling. Callus formation follows the inflammatory phase.

---

## Question 11

A 24-year female with a history of C-section2 weeks back presents to the ED came with complaint of delayed wound healing. What is the most likely cause?

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> | Causes of | e Frequent trauma, Tissue hypoxia and |
> | decreased wound | Infection |
> | healing | Others include: Increased age, |
> |  | immunocompromised and deficiency of vit |
> |  | A, Cand zinc |

- A. Collagen decrease in pregnancy
- B. In this age wound heals slowly
- C. Increased collagen activity
- **D. Infection on the site** (Correct Answer)
- E. Reduced fibroblast

**Correct Answer:** D

**Explanation:**

Infection is the most common cause of delayed wound healing. It causes persistent inflammation, tissue destruction by bacterial proteases, and impairs normal wound healing progression. Other causes include ischaemia, poor nutrition (especially Vitamin C deficiency), diabetes, and corticosteroid use.

---

## Question 12

Which one of the following is not a notifiable disease in the UK?

- A. Covid-19
- B. Small pox
- C. Malaria
- **D. Chicken pox** (Correct Answer)
- E. Measles

**Correct Answer:** D

**Explanation:**

Chickenpox (varicella) is NOT a notifiable disease in England (though it is in Scotland). Notifiable diseases in England include: meningococcal septicaemia, measles, mumps, rubella, TB, cholera, typhoid, etc. Chickenpox is excluded because it is extremely common and the notification would be impractical.

---

## Question 13

A 5-month-old baby with signs of meningitis was brought into the ED. Her mother states that the baby is up to date with immunization schedule. Which one of the following vaccines are not given?

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> | Rotavirus | 2 and 3 months |
> | Hepatitis B | 2, 3, and 4 months |
> | Hib | 2, 3, 4and 12 months (Recall) |
> | Pneumococcal | 3 and 12 months |
> | Diphtheria, polio and tetanus | 2, 3, 4months, preschool and 14 years |
> | MMR | 12 months and preschool (Recall) |
> | MenC | 12 months |
> | MenACWY | 14 years |

- A. Hep-B
- B. Rota virus
- C. Polio
- **D. Men-C** (Correct Answer)
- E. Diphtheria

**Correct Answer:** D

**Explanation:**

The UK immunisation schedule includes MenC vaccine at 12-13 months (not 5 months). The 5-month schedule includes: 6-in-1 (3rd dose), MenB (2nd dose), rotavirus (2nd dose). At 12-13 months: MMR (1st dose), MenB (booster), Hib/MenC. At 5 years: 4-in-1 pre-school booster, MMR (2nd dose).

---

## Question 14

A 27-year-old male presents to the ED with a superficial wound on his right thigh. Your senior registrar decided to let it heal with primary intention. After 2 days which one of the following cells will be predominant?

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> | inflammation |  |
> | immunity |  |
> | Neutrophil | Macrophages and |
> | predominant | lymphocytes |  |
> |  | predominant |
> |  | Note: The granulomatous |
> |  | inflammatory response is |
> |  | a special type of chronic |
> |  | inflammation |
> |  | characterized by often |
> |  | focal collections of |
> |  | macrophages, epithelioid |
> |  | cells and multinucleated |
> |  | giant cells (Recall) |

- A. Macrophages
- B. Lymphocytes
- C. Fibroblasts
- **D. Neutrophils** (Correct Answer)
- E. Eosinophils

**Correct Answer:** D

**Explanation:**

Neutrophils are the predominant cells in the early inflammatory phase of wound healing (day 1-3). They arrive first (within hours) to phagocytose bacteria and debris. Macrophages replace neutrophils by day 3-5 and orchestrate subsequent repair. Lymphocytes arrive later in the proliferative phase.

