## Question 15

A 62-year-old man presents with a stab wound to his scalp. The wound appears superficial but it is bleeding profusely

What pathway is most important for the primary haemostasis of this wound?

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> *(no readable text in the image)*

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> *(no readable text in the image)*

- A. Activation of Protein C
- B. Conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin
- C. Conversion of prothrombin to thrombin
- D. Activation of Protein S
- **E. Activation of von Willebrand factor** (Correct Answer)

**Correct Answer:** E

**Explanation:**

von Willebrand factor (vWF) is essential for primary haemostasis -- it bridges between collagen at the site of vascular injury and platelet GPIb receptors, enabling platelet adhesion. vWF also carries and protects Factor VIII from premature degradation. vWD (von Willebrand Disease) is the most common inherited bleeding disorder.
