## Question 3

A 25-year-old man presents with an incised wound to the antecubital fossa. There is a complete transection of the brachial artery at this level.

Which artery is most likely to remain intact?

- **A. Profunda brachii** (Correct Answer)
- B. Deep palmar arch
- C. Ulnar
- D. Radial
- E. Superficial palmar arch

**Correct Answer:** A

**Explanation:**

Immediately distal to the teres major, the brachial artery gives rise to the profunda brachii (deep artery), which travels with the radial nerve in the radial groove of the humerus and supplies structures in the posterior aspect of the upper arm (e. g., triceps brachii). Incised wound at antecubital fossa and complete transection of brachial artery at this level will spare deep branch of brachial artery that is profunda brachii.

