## Question 11

How is aspirin beneficial in inhibiting platelets aggregation by blocking which of the following pathway?

- A. Inhibition of binding of ADP to its platelet receptor
- B. Inhibition of the breakdown of cAMP
- C. Inhibition of the calcium release
- **D. Inhibition of platelet thromboxane A2 synthesis** (Correct Answer)
- E. Inhibition of GPilb/Illa receptor sites

**Correct Answer:** D

**Explanation:**

Aspirin works as an antiplatelet agent by irreversibly blocking the enzyme cyclooxygenase-1 (COX -1) inside the platelets. This enzyme is necessary t o generate thromboxane A2, a potent platelet activator from arachidonic acid. Aspirin also inhibit s COX-2 which explains part of its anti-inflammatory properties.

Clopidogrel selectively inhibits the binding of adenosine diphosphate (ADP) to its platelet P2Y12 receptor, inhibiting the platelet adhesion and aggregation.

Abciximab binds to the glycoprotein (GP) IIb/IIIareceptor of platelets and inhibits platelet aggregation.

