## Question 10

A 60-year-old patient was involved in a road traffic accident and subsequently complained of unable to perform extension of thigh at hip and flexion at knee joint. Which one of the following nerves is likely affected?

- A. Common peroneal nerve
- **B. Sciatic nerve** (Correct Answer)
- C. Obturator nerve
- D. Femoral nerve
- E. Saphenous nerve

**Correct Answer:** B

**Explanation:**

All the muscles of the posterior compartment of thigh are extensors of thigh at hip and flexors at knee joint. While Biceps femoris is also lateral rotator of thigh at hip joint and Semitendinosus and Semimembranosus also medial rotators of thigh at hip joint

Clinical pearls: All the muscles of the posterior thigh originate from the ischial tuberosity of the pelvis. In some cases, mostly in young athletes, sudden and forceful contraction of the hamstring muscles can result in an avulsion fracture of the ischial tuberosity. It can also be associated with sciatic nerve damage.

