## Question 11

When comparing normally distributed data to negative skewed data on a graph. Which of the following statements are true?

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> | (a) Negatively skewed | (b) Normal (no skew) | (c) Positively skewed |  |
> |  | Mean |  |  |
> |  | Median |  |  |
> | Mode | Mode | Mode |  |
> | Median |  |  | Median |
> | Mean | | |  | Mean |
> |  | The normal curve |  |  |
> | Negative direction | represents a perfectly | Positive direction |  |
> |  | symmetrical distribution |  |  |

- A. Moving away from the peak and toward the tail, the order is always from: mean to mode to median
- B. In left skew the median moves left relative to the mean
- C. In right skew the mean moves left relative to the median
- **D. Moving away from the peak and toward the tail, the order is always from: mode to median to Mean** (Correct Answer)
- E. The median is farther out in the long tail than is the mean

**Correct Answer:** D

**Explanation:**

When comparing normally distributed data to skewed data the following are true:

Moving away from the peak and toward the tail, t he order is always from mode to median to mean.

Skewness is of two types:

In right skew (Positive skew) the mean moves right relative to the median and the tail is longer o n the right side. For a positive skewness mean > median > mode.

In left skew (Negative skew) the mean moves left relative to the median and the tail is longer on left side. For a negative skewness mean < media n < mode (Recall)

