## Question 11

A patient presents with a stab wound to the thenar eminence. Which of the following muscles is most likely involved?

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> | Abductor pollicis brevis | Abducts thumb at MCP) | Median nerve |
> | Flexor pollicis brevis | Flexes thumb at MCPJ | Median nerve |

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> | Abductor digiti minimi | Abducts little finger at MCPJ | Ulnar nerve |
> | Flexor digiti minimi brevis | Flexes little finger at MCPJ | Ulnar nerve |

- A. Lumbricals
- B. Adductor digiit minimi
- C. Extensor pollicis longus
- **D. Abductor pollicis brevis** (Correct Answer)
- E. Flexor pollicis longus

**Correct Answer:** D

**Explanation:**

The thenar eminence is the muscular bulge on t he radial side of the palm, responsible for the movements of the thumb. The muscles contained within the thenar eminence are primarily responsible for the fine motor movements of the thumb and include the abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, and opponens pollicis. Among these, the abductor pollicis brevis is the most superficial and therefore most likely to be involved in a stab wound.

High Yield:

Muscles of the hand:

Thenar muscles:

Three short muscles located in the base of the thumb. All are innervated by median nerve.

Hypothenar muscles:

Hypothenar muscle located at base of middle finger and form hypothenar eminence. All are innervated by ulnar nerve Lumbrical Muscles:

There are four lumbrical muscles. Each one of these originates from a tendon of the flexor digitorum profundus. They pass dorsally and laterally around each finger, and inserts into the extensor hood.

Innervation:

Medial two lumbricals: Ulnar nerve

Lateral two lumbricals: Median nerve

Function:

Flexion at the MCP joint and extension at the interphalangeal (IP) joints of each digit.

