## Question 5

A 29 years old male presents to the ED with complaint of foot drop and loss of sensation on the dorsum of the foot. Which of the following nerve is most likely damaged?

> *Image in the question (OCR text, if any)*
> | Fibularislongus | 1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform |
> |  | (RECALL) |
> | Fibularis brevis | 5th metatarsal |

- A. Sciatic nerve
- **B. Common peroneal nerve** (Correct Answer)
- C. Sural nerve
- D. Posterior tibial nerve
- E. Tibial nerve

**Correct Answer:** B

**Explanation:**

Common fibular nerve wraps around the neck of fibula and enter lateral compartment of leg where it gives rise terminal branches:

Superficial fibular nerve: Runs around lateral compartment of leg and skin over lower anterolateral leg, and dorsum of foot (except skin over webspace of 1st and 2nd toe)

Deep fibular nerve: Anterior compartment of leg and skin over webspace between 1st and 2nd toes (RECALL)

Clinical pearls:

1: Common fibular nerve injury: Damage to the common fibular (peroneal) nerve may occur as a result of fracture of the head or neck of the fibula because it passes behind the head of the fibula and then winds laterally around the neck. T he nerve damage results in foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion) and loss of sensation on the dorsum o f the foot and lateral aspect of the leg and cause s paralysis of all muscles in the anterior and lateral compartments of the leg (dorsiflexors and evertors muscles of the foot).

2: Superficial fibular nerve: May be damaged by fracture of the fibula. As the muscles that the superficial fibular nerve innervates are evertors, injury to the nerve may result in a loss of eversion a nd loss of sensation over the lower anterolateral leg and dorsum of foot.

3: Deep fibular nerve:

Motor Loss: Loss of dorsiflexion of ankle resulting in foot drop. In foot drop foot will appear permanently plantarflexed, foot drop with high-stepping gait, weakness of foot inversion.

Sensory Loss: Loss of sensation over webspace between 1st and 2nd toes.

Note: Footdrop is due to Common fibular or common peroneal nerve or also due to deep fibular nerve injury.

Note: It is very important to remember direct innervation of common fibular nerve.

Deep fibular nerve root: L4-L5 Superficial fibular nerve root: L4-S1.

Muscles of lateral compartment of leg: (High Yield)

