## Question 8

A 33-year-old female presents with complaints of dry eyes and an inability to produce tears when crying. She also reports weakness on the right side of her face, including difficulty closing her right eye and an asymmetric smile. Which nerve is primarily responsible for the parasympathetic innervation of the lacrimal gland?

- A. Trigeminal nerve (CN V)
- B. Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)
- C. Oculomotor nerve (CN III)
- D. Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)
- **E. Facial nerve (CN VII)** (Correct Answer)

**Correct Answer:** E

**Explanation:**

Parasympathetic innervation of the lacrimal gland:

The lacrimal gland is responsible for tear production and is primarily innervated by the parasympathetic fibers of the facial nerve (CN VII). The greater petrosal nerve, a branch of CN VII, carries these preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to th e pterygopalatine ganglion, from where postganglionic fibers travel via the zygomatic branch o f CN V2 (maxillary nerve) and then to the lacrimal nerve (branch of CN V1) to reach the lacrimal gland.

Note: Trigeminal nerve (CN V): lacrimal nerve (branch of CN V1) helps carry parasympathetic fibers to the lacrimal gland, the actual parasympathetic innervation originates from the facial nerve (C N VII).

Sympathetic innervation of the lacrimal gland:

Sympathetic fibres originate from the superior cervical ganglion and are carried by the internal carotid plexus and deep petrosal nerve. They join with the parasympathetic fibres in the nerve of p terygoid canal and follow the same route to supply the gland and inhibits fluid secretion from t he lacrimal gland.

